    },
    {
      "id": "property-template-landing",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Property Template Landing",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -227.08115560388785,
        "y": -581.1368733195291
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 220,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "property-template"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Property Template\n\nA Property has a name, value type, optional default, and a resolved effective value with provenance. Explicit null and unset are different. Enter edit mode and explicitly open the on-node editor.\n\nUse the class bridge to create more Property nodes.\n\n## Events are exposed via ports\n\nThis is how Twilite handles events everywhere, not just here: every Property node intrinsically emits `property.changed` -- declared once, for the whole type, in `eventBridge.js`'s `INTRINSIC_EVENTS_BY_TYPE`, not per instance and not authored on this class at all. That alone gives every Property a real, connectable `event:property.changed` output port, automatically.\n\n**Log Property Change**, below, is wired to that exact port by a real edge into its own `trigger` port (`type: 'listens'`) -- the same mechanism `templates/method-template` demonstrates for a Method-raised event. Editing Example Property's value fires it.\n\nOpen the example to inspect its default and edit its local value."
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1791315217932,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        }
      }
    },
